[
  {
    "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two shallow, matte ceramic bowls placed on a rustic, aged oak wood tabletop, with one bowl in sharp focus in the foreground and the second bowl softly blurred behind it. The camera angle is a 3/4 overhead view (40°), ensuring the full contents of the bowls are clearly visible. Shallow depth of field creates a soft natural bokeh background. Soft natural light flows from a window on the left, casting gentle shadows and highlighting the glossy sheen of the balsamic reduction and olive oil. Inside the bowl, a generous layer of creamy, pearly white 2% cottage cheese sits at the base. Topped precisely over the cheese, but not fully covering it, is a warm mixture of blistered cherry tomatoes—some split and juicy, others holding their shape—and browned, tender artichoke heart pieces. A drizzle of dark, syrupy balsamic vinegar clings to the vegetables, with visible specks of black pepper. The dish is finished with a scatter of vibrant green, roughly chopped fresh basil leaves and coarsely crushed, pale-green pistachios. Beside the bowls, a few warm, toasted whole-wheat pita wedges are stacked casually on the wood. A linen napkin is rumpled beneath one bowl, and a small, stray tomato and a few crushed pistachio fragments rest naturally on the surface. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating—lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a small balsamic drip on the bowl rim, slightly uneven browning on the artichokes. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a crumb trail on the surface, a stray basil leaf on the table. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from a professional cookbook photograph."
  }
]
